      "claim": "The approximate temperatures of the four options are: wax at room temperature ≈ 20 °C, ice‑water mixture ≈ 0 °C, steam from coffee ≈ 100 °C, ice cube in tea ≈ 0 °C.",
      "evidence": "Typical ambient and phase‑change temperatures.",
      "weight": 0.8,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The substances with the lowest temperature are the water in ice water (B) and the ice cube (D), both at about 0 °C.",
      "evidence": "From step 2, 0 °C is the minimum temperature among the options.",
      "weight": 0.7,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "At the same temperature, particles in a solid have less translational kinetic energy than particles in a liquid because they are constrained to vibrate about fixed positions.",
      "evidence": "In solids, molecular motion is primarily vibrational with limited freedom compared to the freer motion in liquids.",
      "weight": 0.6,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore, among the two 0 °C substances, the ice cube (solid) has particles with the lowest kinetic energy.",
      "evidence": "Combines steps 3 and 4.",
